<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Tran dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1407 CE – 1414 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A resistance dynasty of Tran princes who fought the Ming occupation of Vietnam from 1407 until their capture in 1414.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Soon after the Ming conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ho-dynasty">Ho dynasty</a> in 1407, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-dinh-de">Tran Ngoi</a>, a son of the Tran emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-nghe-tong">Tran Nghe Tong</a>, proclaimed himself emperor Gian Dinh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninh-binh-province">Ninh Binh</a> and rallied former Tran officials and generals against the occupiers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Later Tran had no fixed capital and operated as a mobile resistance court in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nghe-an-province">Nghe An</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-hoa">Thanh Hoa</a> and the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-dinh-de">Gian Dinh</a> won a notable victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bo-co">Bo Co</a> in 1408 with the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dang-tat">Dang Tat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-canh-chan">Nguyen Canh Chan</a>, but later had them killed. Their sons then backed Tran Quy Khoang, who became emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-quy-khoang">Trung Quang</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ming forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fu-ming-general">Zhang Fu</a> gradually pushed the Later Tran south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-dinh-de">Gian Dinh</a> was captured in 1409 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-quy-khoang">Trung Quang</a> in 1414, ending the dynasty. Resistance revived four years later under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Le Loi</a>, who succeeded where they had failed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-tran-dynasty.jpg" alt="Later Tran dynasty" width="320" height="496"><figcaption>Image: Hoàng Hữu Xứng (1831-1905) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VietNamToanCanhDuDo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1407 CE – 1414 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gian Dinh De, Trung Quang De</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Vietnam</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hậu Trần</dd></dl></aside></div>
